      "raw_html": "<div class=\"question\" id=\"question-45177\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">1.</span><a class=\"questionKP\" href=\"/topics/2218#2909\">Identifying Why Polygons Are Congruent Using Rigid Motions</a><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"45177\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionGraphicFrame\"><img class=\"questionGraphic\" src=\"/graphics/q-45177\"/></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>In terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"1\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-texatom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-cal mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c50 TEX-C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi data-mjx-variant=\"-tex-calligraphic\" mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi></mrow></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> and <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"2\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-texatom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-cal mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c51 TEX-C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi data-mjx-variant=\"-tex-calligraphic\" mathvariant=\"script\">Q</mi></mrow></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> congruent?</p>\n</div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyEasy\">E</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Wed, Oct 14th @ 1:24 PM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:17</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "question-45302",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214989/assets/8d89477b4c1f7b976f86d1cc5d77c1729826358e1c1367e7982ccd59970c2ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the following combinations of transformations map the triangle $ABC$ to a congruent triangle?\n\nI. A reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a rotation of ${60}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the point $A.$\n\nII. A translation of $2$ units up, followed by a reflection in the line $\\overset{⟷}{BC}.$\n\nIII. A rotation of ${15}^{∘}$ clockwise about a given point $B,$ followed by a translation of $3$ units up.",
      "worked_solution": "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Combination I maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a reflection, followed by a rotation) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination II maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a translation, followed by a reflection) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination III maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a rotation, followed by a translation) are rigid motions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
